y a lot of people in afghanistan  35 million or so were mourning basically  not only girls who were deprived but i  think everyone who had a heart  and who have a heart was  disappointed with the you know with with  the change of position i would say well  the reasoning is that um that the girls  should be um should go to school within  the principles of islam their uniform  should be according to the afghan  cultural cultural values but from my  perspective these are just um excuses um  their sudden decision to revert their  initial announcement indicates that  there is  a division and the taliban are not  you know making decision on a cohesive  basis and there is no chain of command  um you know people at lower level or  ministerial level have a different  um  level of decision making power people at  the higher level have a decision higher  you know they control basically  everything and the important question is  like who is basically the main  decision-making body within taliban that  is something we have been telling um for  the past ten years that who are we  negotiating who is the one making the  decision and this is obvious in in the  case of  taliban not allowing girls to go to  school despite their previous  announcement is that there is a huge  division and they are divided  uh when you speak of a division does  that also imply that therefore there is  at least one part in the taliban that do  want girls to return to school to  secondary schools  they could have many motives one of the  motives is yes because their own girls  go to school  and i have visited their families i have  met their families their girls their  boys um that they go to school that they  their families mainly for most of the  leadership are not in afghanistan they  are living in different countries their  children attend you know high quality  education um and universities one motif  could be that because their own  family members go to school go to work  and they understand the value of  education the second motif could be that  you know because they want to normalize  sort of their relationship with the  world and the world has been emphasizing  so much on girls education  that it has become a matter of  bargaining for for taliban for some  hardliner it's a matter of you know  taking the girls hostage um  so that the world gave them more and for  some others uh who are so to say pro  girls education it's for them to show  that they have changed that they want to  like have a better relationship with the  world so they have different motive but  whatever motive it is i my understanding  is that there is a division and i call  on those actually through this channel  within taliban who who believe that the  girls should go to school should speak  up should raise their voice  because it's not only a matter of girls  education it's for the future of  afghanistan and if they really claim  that they the afghans they belong to the  territory they need to stand now  i'm just curious um and and i'm sorry  i'm belaboring the point but you have  let's call them the pragmatists whose  children go to school abroad uh some of  them girls and then you have the  heartlanders who don't want afghan girls  to go to schools how do the  pragmatists reconcile uh their children  going to school with the views of the  hardliners within the taliban itself  very soon this fragmentation is going to  go  going to evolve further i think um and  those hardliners either will be  marginalized or they will have the whole  control depending on on the situation  one has to see um but my assumption is  that um there is a fragmentation and  that fragmentation was not obvious  during the time that they were small  group fighting in the mountains it's  going to even you know deepen and deepen  with the fact that they are now they're  fighting over power our resources over  everything over issues so this is going  to even deepen uh over time so if the if  the pragmatists are going 